<template name="tus_settings_dspaudio_Monomachine">
<head>
</head>
<body>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<h1>Audio Input A/B</h1>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label id="audioInputStatus"/>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<button id="btAudioInputSettings" class="button">Open Audio/MIDI Settings...</button>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<h1>Host Sync</h1>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<div id="btFollowHostTempo" class="settings-checkboxwithlabel">
		<button id="button" class="settings-checkbox"/>
		<label>Follow host tempo</label>
	</div>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label>The machine takes its tempo, start and stop from the host: it plays when the host plays, in time with it. To set this, the plug-in goes through the Monomachine's GLOBAL menu (CONTROL IN) for a few seconds. Turned off, the machine gets its own tempo back.</label>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label id="hostSyncStatus"/>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<h1>CPU Load</h1>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<div id="btParallelTransport" class="settings-checkboxwithlabel">
		<button id="button" class="settings-checkbox"/>
		<label>Parallel transport (recommended)</label>
	</div>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label>Runs both DSPs on a CPU core of their own, next to the one running the machine's main processor. Together with a latency of 1 or 2 blocks, the emulation then runs outside the host's audio thread, which keeps the DAW's CPU meter low.</label>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label id="parallelTransportStatus"/>
	<settingsspacer1/>
</body>
</template>
